WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:06.220
Today, I'm going to be speaking with Mark
Robert Rank, who is professor of social welfare

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:13.230
at Washington University in St. Louis and
also author of the book Poorly Understood

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.900
Out Now by Oxford University Press.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.150
So great to have you on today.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:18.470
Oh, thanks, David.

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:19.470
Great to be with you.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:25.510
So, I mean, let's just jump into maybe what's
one of the media diving boards into this topic,

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:31.359
maybe which which are the misunderstandings
about the poor in the United States, two of

00:00:31.359 --> 00:00:37.580
which, correct me if I'm wrong, are the misunderstanding
that most poor people don't work and the misunderstanding

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:42.640
that most poor people are minorities, ethnically
or racially.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:47.551
Tell us maybe how this came about, but also
the truth of the statistics.

00:00:47.551 --> 00:00:50.460
Yeah, I think, you know, kind of underlying
those.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:57.280
This is the idea that the myth that poverty
happens to somebody else, it doesn't happen

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:58.280
to me.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:04.751
And so, you know, it happens to folks who
don't work or folks that are minorities, but

00:01:04.751 --> 00:01:06.570
it doesn't really affect me.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:08.680
It's not going to happen to me.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:14.540
And what we show in the book is that actually,
if you look at the MAJAW, the majority of

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:18.150
folks in poverty are actually white.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:24.600
A majority of more folks in poverty live in
suburban areas than in central cities.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.720
And some of the deepest seated poverty is
in rural America.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.810
And the idea that the poor don't work is simply
wrong.

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:34.880
Most of the poor have worked in the past.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.090
It will work in the future.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:42.540
And it's it's really important to think about
poverty is something that people go through.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.250
They're not poor their whole lives.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:47.240
They they hit a spell where they have some
tough times.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:48.670
They experience poverty.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:53.220
They may be out of work, but then they get
back on their feet and they will work in the

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:54.220
future.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:02:00.200
So, you know, I think underlying those ideas
is this myth that poverty is something that

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:04.310
happens to somebody else and doesn't happen
to me.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:07.390
There's a few different things I think would
be interesting to explore.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:11.239
You mentioned suburban versus urban.

00:02:11.239 --> 00:02:16.819
Is there how can we quantify and qualify the
difference between the rural and suburban

00:02:16.819 --> 00:02:21.950
poverty versus the urban poverty, which maybe
is more the caricature of where the myths

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:23.279
come from?

00:02:23.279 --> 00:02:24.319
Yeah.

00:02:24.319 --> 00:02:31.909
So if you you know, it's certainly the case
that folks in inner city central city areas,

00:02:31.909 --> 00:02:36.980
the poverty rates are higher than the overall
rate.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:41.470
But there's many more folks now that live
outside of central cities.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:48.879
And so in terms of the number of folks in
poverty, you know, again, it's higher in surrounding

00:02:48.879 --> 00:02:51.209
urban areas as opposed to the central city.

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:55.860
And as I mentioned, if you look at some of
the most extreme poverty in the United States,

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.239
it's actually found in rural America.

00:02:58.239 --> 00:03:05.669
So areas like Appalachia, the Deep South and
Mississippi Delta, American Indian reservations,

00:03:05.669 --> 00:03:10.279
the central corridor of California, all of
those areas have really, really deep seated

00:03:10.279 --> 00:03:11.279
poverty.

00:03:11.279 --> 00:03:16.169
And that's kind of when we will have this
image of of who are the poor.

00:03:16.169 --> 00:03:20.980
We often think about the poor as nonwhites
in inner city areas.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:27.290
And it's not to discount that that poverty
exists there, of course, but poverty is much,

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:28.290
much wider.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:34.319
The way I like to describe it is that the
reach of poverty is really wide, but its grip

00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:36.359
is is not that strong.

00:03:36.359 --> 00:03:38.889
People move in and out of poverty a lot.

00:03:38.889 --> 00:03:45.409
What are the most common reasons that people
who at some point were not poor end up in

00:03:45.409 --> 00:03:52.080
poverty, which maybe is different from transgenerational
poverty, where you're born into poverty and

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:53.449
remain there?

00:03:53.449 --> 00:03:55.760
Is there an interesting distinction to be
made here?

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:56.760
There is.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:05.099
So the folks that are long term in poverty
is about 10 to 15 percent of everybody who

00:04:05.099 --> 00:04:07.060
will experience poverty so far.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:11.870
Eighty five percent of people who experience
poverty do so for a fairly short period of

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:12.870
time.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.650
And by short, I mean a year or two.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:17.639
Now, why do they experience poverty?

00:04:17.639 --> 00:04:22.910
It's because things happen to people that
they didn't anticipate, like losing a job,

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:27.220
a family splitting up, getting sick, a pandemic
occurring.

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:32.590
All of these things have the potential to
throw people into poverty for a short period,

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.490
a fairly short period of time.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:38.710
Now, what's interesting about this is I've
done a lot of work and and the book focuses

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:44.440
on this right from the get go of the question
of what's the long term likelihood that an

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:47.380
American at some point will experience poverty.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:48.380
Right.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:54.610
It turns out that between the ages of 20 and
75, 60 percent of Americans will experience

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:59.629
one year below the official poverty line and
three quarters will experience poverty or

00:04:59.629 --> 00:05:00.629
near poverty.

00:05:00.629 --> 00:05:05.720
And the reason is because, again, over a long
period of time, things happen to people that

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:06.910
can throw them into poverty.

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:11.199
And when that happens, there's not a lot in
place to protect them.

00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:19.860
How how much or is that number at all skewed
to people in their very early 20s in college

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:24.940
who are technically in poverty, but it's not
really reflective of their situation?

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:26.860
Yeah, that's a great that's a great question.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:29.880
And that's a question that we often get on
that research.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:36.449
And so what we did is we took out those folks
and did the analysis and found that it it

00:05:36.449 --> 00:05:38.569
varied.

00:05:38.569 --> 00:05:39.680
It didn't change very much.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:45.050
I mean, the number of people that are actually
in graduate school that might sort of fit

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:49.460
that sort of starving student picture is actually
pretty small.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:50.460
So it doesn't.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:54.370
Really affect the numbers very much at all,
graduate school and presumably college juniors

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:55.370
and seniors, right?

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:56.550
I mean, 20, 20 years.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:59.160
Yeah, it's so it's really it's not a huge
doesn't change the numbers dramatically.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.130
It doesn't change that when you again, when
you look over a 30 or 40 year period, it really

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:09.520
doesn't change it very much when
it comes to the I mean, I it may go back before

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:10.520
this, actually.

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:13.099
Maybe that's a good good question for me.

00:06:13.099 --> 00:06:19.330
Some of the big historical markers of when
the narrative around the poor started to resemble

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:20.330
what it is now.

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:26.510
I think back to the Reagan era, the story
of, you know, the welfare taking Cadillac

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:28.761
queen, having kids in order to get food stamps.

00:06:28.761 --> 00:06:34.650
And how far back does that trope sort of go?

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:39.870
Well, I think you're right that the kind of
the modern version of that really started

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:44.210
with Ronald Reagan, but the idea of the deserving
and the undeserving poor actually goes back

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:49.169
400 years to sort of England's poor laws at
the time.

00:06:49.169 --> 00:06:54.820
But certainly Ronald Reagan was the one in
more modern times that really emphasized that.

00:06:54.820 --> 00:07:03.150
And the argument that he's make it was making
was using those stereotypes to argue for smaller

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:04.150
government.

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:09.520
You know, his big line, one of his lines was,
you know, government is not the the answer

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:13.999
government is the problem that we fought a
war on poverty and poverty won.

00:07:13.999 --> 00:07:19.520
Well, it turns out that well constructed government
programs actually have a very significant

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:21.699
effect on reducing poverty.

00:07:21.699 --> 00:07:26.919
So take the war on poverty in nineteen fifty
nine, the poverty rate in the United States

00:07:26.919 --> 00:07:28.680
was around 22 percent.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.409
By nineteen seventy three, it was 11 percent.

00:07:31.409 --> 00:07:36.819
We cut poverty in half because of the war
on poverty and because of the strong economic

00:07:36.819 --> 00:07:38.699
growth during that time.

00:07:38.699 --> 00:07:40.710
So did we eliminate poverty?

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:46.319
No, but it's an example that government actually
can be quite effective in reducing poverty.

00:07:46.319 --> 00:07:53.090
So in terms of reducing poverty, I mean, there
are some poverty inducing factors that are

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:58.249
similar around the world and there are some
that are that are less common, you know, medical

00:07:58.249 --> 00:07:59.249
expense.

00:07:59.249 --> 00:08:01.849
Bankruptcy doesn't exist in many countries.

00:08:01.849 --> 00:08:03.430
It does exist in the United States.

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:09.319
So this understanding that this may be hard
to parse out when we talk about poverty, discussions

00:08:09.319 --> 00:08:15.770
often include all of the following and more
minimum wage, access to health care, improving

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:22.990
education, cost of living, rising wage gap,
you on and on and on.

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:28.499
What interventions have been shown to be the
most effective at cutting poverty?

00:08:28.499 --> 00:08:39.390
Well, I think the number one number one solution
is jobs that can pay a decent wage, jobs that

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:43.990
provide a livable wage and also having benefits
associated with those.

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:49.420
So what has happened in the United States
over the last 40 or 50 years is we've been

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:55.030
producing more and more low wage jobs in jobs
that don't provide benefits.

00:08:55.030 --> 00:09:02.170
And so a median male worker who is full time
in the labor market today is earning slightly

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:07.310
less once you control for inflation than he
was earning in 1973.

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:12.190
There's been a huge stagnation, and that's
one of the reasons why more and more people

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:16.880
are at risk of poverty because they're working
at jobs that just can't support a family.

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:20.510
So I think that's the number one factor that
we need to focus on.

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:25.290
But as you point out, there are many other
factors as well, such as having access to

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:28.540
health care, child care, good education.

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:33.040
Those are all really key elements to addressing
the issue of poverty.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:38.840
When we think about stimulus, like the kind
we've seen now three times during the course

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:45.290
of the pandemic, or a proposal for a 15 dollar
minimum wage, which the sort of more free

00:09:45.290 --> 00:09:53.260
market minded folks would say these are these
are artificial decisions that are being imposed.

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:57.480
The minimum wage would be a price floor on
labor that might be above what the market

00:09:57.480 --> 00:09:58.480
would dictate.

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:03.570
Or just dumping a one time payment on people
is not really going to systemically change

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:07.560
the value of their work to an employer or
whatever the case may be.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:11.910
What do we know about the longer term impact
of those types of interventions?

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:13.590
Yeah, interesting.

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:20.650
So first of all, the idea of providing kind
of a monthly universal payment to families

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:25.510
with children has been going on in European
countries for decades.

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:29.650
And it's shown that that's really, really
effective in terms of reducing the rate of

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:31.540
childhood poverty.

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:38.790
And in the end, President Biden's pandemic
package, we have a version of that where every

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:43.330
family with a child would get three or four
hundred dollars a month.

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:49.710
So we are actually starting to get some research
in on the effect of this type of a of a policy.

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:50.710
There's a.

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:55.410
There was a study that just came out from
Stockton, California, that did kind of a universal

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:59.780
basic income and they actually showed it had
really positive effects.

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:06.080
And interestingly, it actually the folks that
were getting the UBI actually had higher rates

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:10.980
of employment than those who weren't, because
that's one of the fears that all of this creates

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:12.330
a work disincentive.

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:18.200
Actually, it creates a lot of positive incentives
in terms of people being able to live what

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:21.660
I often refer to as a livable life.

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:25.720
The mobility question is an interesting one,
where there's a bunch of ways to measure how

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:29.500
likely it is that if born into poverty, one
will exit poverty.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:33.720
And you can look at this at a country level,
you can pass it out by education and race

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:38.630
and all of these different things for those
who are born into poverty.

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:40.080
But get out of it.

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:45.760
What do we know about the following generation,
the kids of those folks and their likelihood

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:48.170
of remaining out of poverty as well?

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:49.170
Yeah.

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:55.010
So the way I like to think about this is that
it's the notion of cumulative inequality.

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:59.380
So first of all, as I said, most folks who
experience poverty do so fairly short periods

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:03.010
of time, but they may not get that far above
the poverty line.

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:07.940
Yeah, but the way the way we we often like
to think about the United States is that there's

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:11.960
a level playing field and everybody's kind
of starting at the same point.

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:18.310
And I use the analogy in the book of actually
what's going on is a modified game of Monopoly.

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:20.070
Oh, this is interesting.

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:23.510
Now, I've used this I think I've used this
analogy before.

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:26.430
And I'm wondering where you first heard it,
actually.

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:28.510
Well, I thought I came up with it.

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:29.510
Oh.

00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:30.510
Oh, that's interesting.

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:31.510
Wow.

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:32.510
I don't know.

00:12:32.510 --> 00:12:34.040
Will tell it to us because let's see if it's
the same one I use.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:35.040
Yeah.

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:37.880
OK, so we normally think of a game of Monopoly.

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:39.170
We have three players.

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:40.810
Everybody starts out with fifteen hundred
dollars.

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:41.810
Yeah.

00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:48.020
They go around the rules of the game apply
and you know, luck and chance and skill determine

00:12:48.020 --> 00:12:49.530
who is going to win the game.

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:55.180
But now let's look at an altered game of Monopoly
where player one starts out with five thousand

00:12:55.180 --> 00:12:56.460
dollars.

00:12:56.460 --> 00:13:00.150
Player two starts out with the fifteen hundred
dollars and player three starts out with two

00:13:00.150 --> 00:13:01.200
hundred fifty dollars.

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:07.090
Now we're still going to have the same rules
and and abide by those.

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:09.380
But who's going to win most of those games?

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:16.350
Well, given the prior advantages, player one
is going to win considerably more even if

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:19.090
he or she is not as lucky or as skilled.

00:13:19.090 --> 00:13:25.360
OK, and the other point is that player one
can play the game differently than player

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:26.490
three.

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:28.400
They can take more chances.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:31.980
If player three makes one mistake, it's over.

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:35.970
And that's what's really going on in this
country, is we have an alternate game of Monopoly

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:40.440
where some players are starting out with that
five thousand dollars more and more players

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:43.500
are starting out with maybe a thousand or
fifteen hundred.

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:48.070
And unfortunately, many people are starting
out with the two hundred and fifty dollars.

00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:54.470
So we really we need to think about how can
we address some of these prior advantages

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:59.600
and disadvantages so that we have more of
a sense of the quality of opportunity which

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:02.110
we often talk about as important in the United
States.

00:14:02.110 --> 00:14:03.110
Right.

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:04.480
So we're at the end of our time.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:08.260
But so my version of the analogy is, yeah,
it's almost the same.

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:11.180
It's everybody really does get the same amount
of cash.

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:15.740
But the late starting players have to contend
with all the properties already having hotels

00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:16.750
on them.

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:17.750
Absolutely.

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:20.470
And so but it's really the same idea that's
very high.

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:21.470
Yeah.

00:14:21.470 --> 00:14:22.470
Yeah.

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:25.450
No, that that
is we've been speaking with Mark Robert, Rank,

00:14:25.450 --> 00:14:29.800
professor of social welfare at Washington
University in St. Louis.

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:36.280
The book is poorly understood, which we will
link to in the YouTube video for for our conversation

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:37.280
today.

00:14:37.280 --> 00:14:38.640
Mark, really a pleasure having you on.

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:40.060
Oh, this was great, Dave and I really enjoyed
it.

